1
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:23,390
[nm]: Well, the reason... the reason why it's... the reason why understanding is hard to develop, because in order to do it, one needs to know what must be understood.

2
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:35,280
[nm]: So, it might sound like self-explanatory, but the point is, it's not... understanding takes repetition.

3
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:38,990
[nm]: So, one needs to make the effort of drilling,

4
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:46,380
[nm]: a topic, a theme that needs to be clarified, that one wants to develop clarity in regard to.

5
00:00:47,010 --> 00:00:50,550
[nm]: And in order to do that, you need to know what to look for.

6
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:54,340
[nm]: You need to know what... how to... how to approach it and what to understand.

7
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:57,440
[nm]: So, getting information about it, it's absolutely necessary.

8
00:00:58,990 --> 00:01:05,840
[nm]: But, information in itself, as vast as it can be, that is not understanding.

9
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:05,860
[nm]: And that's also another thing that sometimes people would confuse.
[nm]: But, information in itself, as vast as it can be, that is not understanding.

10
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:08,650
[nm]: And that's also another thing that sometimes people would confuse.

11
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:20,380
[nm]: Knowing a lot about something does not mean you understood what that means, especially when it comes down to nature of things, the nature of feelings, the nature of intentions, the nature of experience as a whole.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:34,350
[nm]: So, when it comes to practicing the dhamma, see, most people would either just mystify, mystify it, as in go after

13
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:45,090
[nm]: after the experience of novelty, or some energy release, or something new, and then they would refer to that as understanding.

14
00:01:45,870 --> 00:01:59,800
[nm]: But if you cannot explain an experience, whatever that is, to yourself at least, or make it intelligible to yourself or to somebody else, you should not think you understood it.

15
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:02,300
[nm]: Now, of course, you might be...

16
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:05,730
[nm]: You're free to use your own words, so to speak.

17
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:08,940
[nm]: You don't have to use templates of descriptions and so on.

18
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:16,430
[nm]: But the point is, you will still be able to convey the meaning, if you understood that experience in its nature.

19
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:18,410
[nm]: And that's the crucial factor here.

20
00:02:18,530 --> 00:02:20,710
[nm]: Meaning of the experience.

21
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:31,900
[nm]: Meaning of the whatever it might have happened to you, that you think was somehow worthy in your practice, in your meditation, or in any other area of life for that matter.

22
00:02:33,110 --> 00:02:41,920
[nm]: And that's when you realize that most of the time, actually, even things you think you understood, upon which you act, and you choose, and you live your life, you haven't actually understood the meaning behind.

23
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:42,690
[nm]: You just do it.

24
00:02:43,270 --> 00:02:45,460
[nm]: It made sense, or what is the Buddha said?

25
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:50,090
[nm]: You would be doing these things, or holding those views, simply because it pleases you.

26
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:53,320
[nm]: That's the sole criteria for most people.

27
00:02:53,320 --> 00:03:00,560
[nm]: I find the idea of this pleasing, and that's why I defend that view, and that's why I defend these actions, or I do them, and so on.

28
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:04,830
[nm]: Either way, there is an understanding of those same things.

29
00:03:04,830 --> 00:03:08,570
[nm]: And that's the important difference that we're trying to highlight here, basically.

30
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:13,000
[nm]: It's not... it's not about the experience that you might have, or you might get.

31
00:03:13,230 --> 00:03:14,820
[nm]: It's about understanding it.

32
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:14,830
[nm]: And if understanding is the focus,
[nm]: It's about understanding it.

33
00:03:14,830 --> 00:03:16,780
[nm]: And if understanding is the focus,

34
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:21,920
[nm]: that's when you realize the content of the experience doesn't matter as much.

35
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:28,520
[nm]: As long as it's wholesome, as long as it's based on restraint and virtue, then you realize it's the understanding that matters.

36
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:31,250
[nm]: Understanding of any experience.

37
00:03:32,290 --> 00:03:34,170
[nm]: That's what results in freedom from suffering.

38
00:03:34,170 --> 00:03:36,600
[nm]: That's what results in fulfilling the practice of dhamma.

39
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:46,990
[nm]: And, eh... and that's what the Buddha said himself, like, eh... when he said whether the five aggregates, when they're fully understood, one is... one is finished, finished the work.

40
00:03:47,370 --> 00:03:48,650
[nm]: And he said, it doesn't matter...

41
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:52,230
[nm]: So, it doesn't matter what... what, eh... content of the five aggregates.

42
00:03:52,230 --> 00:04:03,390
[nm]: He would... he would say, it doesn't matter if past, future, present, far, near, up, down, below, behind, any shape or form, it's going to be within the experience as a whole, which means going to be within the five aggregates.

43
00:04:03,730 --> 00:04:07,170
[nm]: And that is something that, eh... a person has understood.

44
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:28,170
[nm]: So, the reason why this practice is harder than to just sit and sort of try to develop calmness or some pleasant abiding through the practice of concentration is because it can result, it will result initially in sort of causing doubt, restlessness in your mind and that's how you do it properly.

45
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:36,500
[nm]: By working through these things whereby you learn to be imperturbable in the face of the thinking mind.

46
00:04:37,290 --> 00:04:38,540
[nm]: Not other way around.

47
00:04:38,770 --> 00:04:44,570
[nm]: You're afraid of the thinking mind, because it disturbs you, and then you try to stop the thinking.

48
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:50,770
[nm]: You try to concentrate on a little point, so you don't have to think about anything else.

49
00:04:51,450 --> 00:05:00,160
[nm]: It's you who should develop strength, in order to be imperturbable, not control the content that disturbs you, that perturbs you.

50
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:08,390
[nm]: So, it's important to then, obviously, read the suttas and get the idea as to what needs to be understood.

51
00:05:08,730 --> 00:05:14,880
[nm]: But, very often, it comes down to that tedious, ambiguous, heavy drilling.

52
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:16,380
[nm]: Drilling of your thought.

53
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:18,130
[nm]: Like, you sit down...

54
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:28,230
[nm]: I don't know, if you read the sutta that was talking about, for example, feelings and intentions, say intentions, you sit down and ask yourself.

55
00:05:28,230 --> 00:05:29,380
[nm]: I mean, obviously, sit down quietly.

56
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:30,250
[nm]: Don't be in a...

57
00:05:31,070 --> 00:05:44,570
[nm]: in a crowded environment and so on, just have some environmental control, so to speak, there won't be any disturbances, phones ringing and so on, and ask yourself, intention, intention, what is intention?

58
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:47,620
[nm]: Do I know what intention is?

59
00:05:47,670 --> 00:05:53,980
[nm]: For me, for myself, in my own experience, when I say intention to you or to anybody, what do I refer to?

60
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:56,690
[nm]: Is it understood?

61
00:05:56,690 --> 00:05:57,580
[nm]: Is it... what is it?

62
00:05:58,430 --> 00:06:05,900
[nm]: Whatever it is, do I understand it or I just go sort of float on the surface of the meaning of intention, vague meaning that I kind of have?

63
00:06:06,790 --> 00:06:08,080
[nm]: How would you explain intention?

64
00:06:08,470 --> 00:06:09,730
[nm]: Try, try write it down.

65
00:06:09,890 --> 00:06:10,710
[nm]: What is intention?

66
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:15,700
[nm]: And then afterwards read that and see if it makes any sense to you.

67
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:18,510
[nm]: If it does, then push the questions further.

68
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:21,510
[nm]: So, in this case, you ask yourself, what is intention?

69
00:06:21,510 --> 00:06:22,930
[nm]: What is my intention?

70
00:06:22,930 --> 00:06:28,820
[nm]: Like now, I have intention to be seated here, and contemplate on the nature of intention.

71
00:06:30,090 --> 00:06:32,260
[nm]: What is that intention that's making me do this?

72
00:06:33,270 --> 00:06:33,680
[nm]: What is it?

73
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:35,350
[nm]: Is it a fleeting thought?

74
00:06:36,610 --> 00:06:39,580
[nm]: Well, it isn't, because I'm still seated, trying to contemplate these things.

75
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:42,470
[nm]: Which means, that thought is present.

76
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:51,620
[nm]: Throughout these more particular actions that I'm doing, that I'm trying to contemplate, I remain seated, I still haven't switched on my phone, I still haven't opened my door to the world.

77
00:06:52,330 --> 00:07:06,490
[nm]: So, all these other things I do, I do through or on the basis of simultaneously present, more general intention, which is to ponder on the nature of intention, or the nature of feeling, or the nature of five aggregates.

78
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:11,970
[nm]: So, how else would you then describe that presently enduring...

79
00:07:12,910 --> 00:07:13,620
[nm]: intention.

80
00:07:15,570 --> 00:07:16,450
[nm]: Is it a thought?

81
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,280
[nm]: Is it some sort of... is it perceived?

82
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:23,120
[nm]: It is, because if it weren't, you wouldn't be able to talk about it.

83
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:27,080
[nm]: You wouldn't be able to... to... to designate it even for yourself, let alone for others.

84
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:29,610
[nm]: So, it's a... it's a peripheral thought.

85
00:07:29,610 --> 00:07:29,620
[nm]: It's... you're not... See, when you're... like in this case, we have intention to sit down and contemplate.
[nm]: So, it's a... it's a peripheral thought.

86
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:34,850
[nm]: It's... you're not... See, when you're... like in this case, we have intention to sit down and contemplate.

87
00:07:35,570 --> 00:07:36,840
[nm]: You're not seated...

88
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:47,130
[nm]: You don't have to keep it in front of your mind, as in, while you're sitting, while you're contemplating, you don't have to keep saying to yourself: I want to sit, I want to sit, I want to contemplate, that's my intention.

89
00:07:47,530 --> 00:07:50,250
[nm]: No, because these actions already carry that intention.

90
00:07:50,260 --> 00:07:51,690
[nm]: That's how intentions work.

91
00:07:51,940 --> 00:08:03,540
[nm]: That's why I keep saying the most important thing for somebody who wants to start the practice, or somebody who is already developing, is to develop full transparency in regard to presently enduring motivations behind their smallest action.

92
00:08:04,630 --> 00:08:06,110
[nm]: That's how you get to discern.

93
00:08:06,910 --> 00:08:19,560
[nm]: The fundamental aspect of wholesome or unwholesome, bad and good, good and bad, and especially to discern where your actions are rooted at.

94
00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:33,010
[nm]: Because if you're not aware of this peripheral thought of your intention that sort of directs all your particular actions while it endures, you're not aware of it, of where that action, those actions are rooted.

95
00:08:33,590 --> 00:08:38,000
[nm]: That's why intention is wholesome, unwholesome, or it can be neutral as well.

96
00:08:38,890 --> 00:08:42,590
[nm]: But, for practical sake, just look for wholesome and unwholesome.

97
00:08:42,590 --> 00:08:44,210
[nm]: If it's unwholesome, avoid it.

98
00:08:44,970 --> 00:08:47,400
[nm]: If it's wholesome, don't try to deny it or fear it.

99
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:48,620
[nm]: Just... just let it be.

100
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:49,280
[nm]: Let it endure.

101
00:08:49,650 --> 00:08:51,120
[nm]: Either way, they arise on their own.

102
00:08:51,430 --> 00:08:54,630
[nm]: But your responsibility is not try to prevent them from arising.

103
00:08:54,630 --> 00:08:58,840
[nm]: Your responsibility is to try to prevent yourself from acting out of it.

104
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:02,700
[nm]: So, as I said, even if you're a beginner,

105
00:09:03,770 --> 00:09:20,060
[nm]: Sit down, quietly, semi-protected environment, you're not distracted, and pick up things you're doing, pick up things you want to do, bring them forward to your mind, and then think about why, why you want to do it.

106
00:09:21,330 --> 00:09:22,750
[nm]: Oh, it's because of this, this and this.

107
00:09:22,750 --> 00:09:26,690
[nm]: Yeah, well, why is this, this and this a reason for me to do these things?

108
00:09:28,290 --> 00:09:29,990
[nm]: Why did I choose that to be the reason?

109
00:09:29,990 --> 00:09:30,720
[nm]: It's always on you.

110
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:32,710
[nm]: Intention is always your responsibility.

111
00:09:32,710 --> 00:09:35,880
[nm]: That's why the Buddha said every action is intention.

112
00:09:36,010 --> 00:09:41,250
[nm]: You can't commit an action without having it been rooted in intention to commit that action.

113
00:09:41,830 --> 00:09:44,120
[nm]: And that's why beings are owners of their action.

114
00:09:44,630 --> 00:09:45,640
[nm]: Only you, nobody else.

115
00:09:45,640 --> 00:09:48,010
[nm]: Which means you are fundamentally always responsible.

116
00:09:48,530 --> 00:09:50,170
[nm]: It's not negotiable or optional.

117
00:09:51,250 --> 00:09:56,380
[nm]: So, you sit down here, quietly, ask yourself what is the nature... what is intention?

118
00:09:56,570 --> 00:10:00,690
[nm]: When I say intention, intention, what am I referring to in this experience as a whole?

119
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:05,770
[nm]: So, we kind of established it's a sort of ambiguous peripheral thought.

120
00:10:05,900 --> 00:10:09,170
[nm]: The idea, a context of all these little things I'm doing.

121
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:13,140
[nm]: You don't have to keep staring at little things that you're doing in order to discern the intention.

122
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:13,400
[nm]: Why?

123
00:10:13,410 --> 00:10:15,900
[nm]: Because the intention always comes first, structurally.

124
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:26,690
[nm]: Simultaneously, intentions and actions through your intentions are present, but intention is... is... is more... more... more... more... it's deeper, so to speak.

125
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:29,420
[nm]: It's not that you have the intention and then the actions...

126
00:10:29,420 --> 00:10:33,680
[nm]: You have intentions and you have your environment in regard to which you have intentions.

127
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:41,480
[nm]: But that's when you recognize that these intentions determine the course of my action and for that I'm responsible for saying yes or no to it.

128
00:10:43,140 --> 00:10:50,320
[nm]: So, we establish that intention in the most fundamental, most basic, the most straightforward description.

129
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:51,640
[nm]: It's a thought.

130
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:54,900
[nm]: Right thought, bad thought, basically.

131
00:10:55,460 --> 00:10:56,560
[nm]: So, you have a thought

132
00:10:56,890 --> 00:10:59,290
[nm]: that gives the context to your present actions.

133
00:10:59,810 --> 00:11:06,150
[nm]: But if you're unaware of that thought, means you're unaware of the nature of it, whether the nature is wholesome or unwholesome.

134
00:11:06,780 --> 00:11:13,370
[nm]: And if that is the case, that means you will not know whether your actions are rooted in good or bad.

135
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:19,970
[nm]: Through avoiding responsibility for your intentions, you end up avoiding responsibility for your actions down the line.

136
00:11:20,950 --> 00:11:25,790
[nm]: That's why the five precepts, the eight precepts, that'swhywhere sīla and virtue always has to come first.

137
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:34,770
[nm]: Like, you can avoid responsibility only... you're allowed to... well, not allowed to, but you... you have to start narrowing down your avoidance of responsibility.

138
00:11:34,770 --> 00:11:40,670
[nm]: Someone who doesn't keep the precepts, he avoids responsibility left and right, and there is no basis for dhamma whatsoever.

139
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:45,880
[nm]: Then, you start keeping the precepts, five precepts, eight precepts or more, that's where you create a basis.

140
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:48,770
[nm]: Okay, I might avoid responsibility here and there,

141
00:11:49,790 --> 00:12:01,580
[nm]: Until I work, work, invest enough work into that, and rectify it, but at least I will not avoid responsibility past this point, past the five precepts, or eight, and so on.

142
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:02,900
[nm]: So, that's it.

143
00:12:02,900 --> 00:12:13,060
[nm]: You just kind of start rounding up that avoidance of responsibility for your actions, that they can clarify your responsibility for your actions, that that can then bring further clarity that you're actually...

144
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:24,070
[nm]: Your responsibility goes even beyond that and it rests on the responsibility for welcoming the thought of greed, the thought of aversion, ill-will, cruelty, the thought of distraction.

145
00:12:24,570 --> 00:12:40,240
[nm]: That's why the Buddha would say the noble disciple, the one with the right view, who has developed clarity in regard to the nature of his intentions, he... all he has to do is not tolerate and not delight and not welcome the unwholesome thoughts.

146
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:46,120
[nm]: And all he has to do is not be ignorant and distract himself from wholesome thoughts.

147
00:12:46,420 --> 00:12:52,140
[nm]: So, when unwholesome intentions arise, he's aware of it, but he doesn't need to do anything.

148
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:55,420
[nm]: All he needs to do is abstain from doing anything on account of it.

149
00:12:55,430 --> 00:13:01,880
[nm]: So, he knows, I will not welcome this and I will not entertain this thought to stay even a second longer.

150
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:08,840
[nm]: If that attitude is present, he will be unable to act out of it.

151
00:13:08,890 --> 00:13:09,970
[nm]: That's just impossible.

152
00:13:10,260 --> 00:13:17,170
[nm]: The only way you can act out of unwholesome intentions is if you ignore them to be unwholesome.

153
00:13:17,490 --> 00:13:20,540
[nm]: As soon as you're aware of their unwholesomeness, you cannot act out of it.

154
00:13:20,910 --> 00:13:22,250
[nm]: So it's mutually exclusive.

155
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:25,360
[nm]: You must ignore unwholesome in order to act out of it.

156
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:33,400
[nm]: And that's exactly why ignorance is the most blameworthy and it's kind of a fundamental prerequisite for anything unwholesome.

157
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:36,420
[nm]: And also, I guess you could mention that,

158
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:44,370
[nm]: ignorance, as you can see now, it's not... it's not a metaphysical sort of secret, mystery.

159
00:13:44,770 --> 00:13:46,280
[nm]: It's this very act of ignoring.

160
00:13:46,290 --> 00:13:53,430
[nm]: Ignoring responsibility, avoiding transparency, avoiding the recognition of your choice in regard to the present intentions.

161
00:13:53,950 --> 00:13:55,030
[nm]: That's ignorant.

162
00:13:55,030 --> 00:13:58,750
[nm]: That's why you carry it, you perpetuate it through your own actions.

163
00:13:58,980 --> 00:14:03,290
[nm]: Ignorant actions perpetuate ignorance because of which actions are ignorant.

164
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:05,050
[nm]: So, it's a vicious circle.

165
00:14:05,910 --> 00:14:13,630
[nm]: But you can break through it, if you start accepting that responsibility, if you start looking at the motivation behind your action and then developing clarity in regard to its nature.

166
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:16,260
[nm]: As I said, what is the nature of intention?

167
00:14:16,320 --> 00:14:18,130
[nm]: When I say intention, what do I refer to it?

168
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:21,710
[nm]: Do I refer to a specific thought or general thoughts?

169
00:14:22,030 --> 00:14:23,570
[nm]: Do I refer to a mood, a feeling?

170
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:24,260
[nm]: What is it?

171
00:14:27,210 --> 00:14:30,690
[nm]: The main problem is not necessarily not being able to give an answer right away.

172
00:14:31,330 --> 00:14:32,490
[nm]: The main problem is not even

173
00:14:33,610 --> 00:14:47,230
[nm]: attempting to think about it in the first place, which is what most people do, because there is... it's so ambiguous initially, that you... our thinking is used to thinking about the positive categories, things that are in front of you.

174
00:14:47,860 --> 00:15:00,380
[nm]: So, you trying to step back from that, without losing sight of it, you trying to step back from that, without losing sight of it, it's going to take quite some effort, because we are used to just being focused on the content.

175
00:15:01,860 --> 00:15:05,840
[nm]: But now you have to be discerning the nature in regard to the present content.

176
00:15:06,420 --> 00:15:19,260
[nm]: So that's that's why this kind of thinking requires constant constant drilling... and lots of setups and prerequisite of virtue and sense restraint in order to take the route.

177
00:15:20,130 --> 00:15:21,860
[nm]: Otherwise, it just it just won't happen.

178
00:15:21,860 --> 00:15:24,660
[nm]: You're just floating from one content on to the other.

179
00:15:26,050 --> 00:15:27,200
[nm]: So you can pick up anything.

180
00:15:27,210 --> 00:15:30,100
[nm]: You can also pick up like the aggregated feeling, for example.

181
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:34,190
[nm]: And ask yourself, what do I... what do I feel right now?

182
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:35,660
[nm]: What is the nature of the feeling?

183
00:15:35,660 --> 00:15:36,700
[nm]: How could I describe it?

184
00:15:37,570 --> 00:15:38,260
[nm]: Oh, is it like this?

185
00:15:38,260 --> 00:15:38,790
[nm]: Is it like that?

186
00:15:38,790 --> 00:15:40,030
[nm]: Yeah, but what am I describing now?

187
00:15:40,040 --> 00:15:44,760
[nm]: If I say, oh, I feel light and... well, are those feelings or those perceptions of lightness and so on?

188
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:48,930
[nm]: Feeling is, as the suttas say, pleasant, unpleasant, neutral.

189
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:58,400
[nm]: So, all these perceptions I'm talking about, I feel light, I feel heavy, I feel this, I feel that, I feel... Where would they be rooted, fundamentally, presently?

190
00:15:58,470 --> 00:16:00,240
[nm]: Is it pleasant, unpleasant or neutral?

191
00:16:00,740 --> 00:16:02,090
[nm]: So, that's how you start clarifying the feeling.

192
00:16:02,090 --> 00:16:09,090
[nm]: You realize most of the often... most often things we think feelings are, it's actually talking about perceptions and thoughts and concepts.

193
00:16:09,690 --> 00:16:14,360
[nm]: And you will be talking about those things for as long as you don't make the effort to think about what the actual feeling is.

194
00:16:15,350 --> 00:16:29,610
[nm]: So, you could see that practice of right understanding, practice of developing the right view, it's not about, as I said, building on this great system that will then culminate in release of energy and some mystical experience through a practice and effort.

195
00:16:29,830 --> 00:16:34,160
[nm]: It's really about undoing the state of puthujjana, which is the starting point for everyone.

196
00:16:34,810 --> 00:16:37,420
[nm]: So, it's not building something new on top of the state of puthujjana.

197
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:40,670
[nm]: It's just simply obliterating everything that makes you puthujjana.

198
00:16:40,940 --> 00:16:42,410
[nm]: That's how you become a sotāpanna.

199
00:16:43,020 --> 00:16:48,270
[nm]: So, lack of understanding, replaced with understanding, means all the wrong views are gone.

200
00:16:48,660 --> 00:16:50,420
[nm]: Lack of understanding in regard to what?

201
00:16:50,420 --> 00:16:52,080
[nm]: In regard to the nature of your experience.

202
00:16:52,460 --> 00:16:58,940
[nm]: In regard to the five aggregates, in regard to the suffering, freedom from it, in regard to... pick up any topic from the sutta.

203
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:08,650
[nm]: Fully understand it, you will... you would have... you would have then undone your... your ignorance that was keeping you... that was maintaining you, eh, puthujjana.

204
00:17:09,810 --> 00:17:12,270
[nm]: That's just... that's just how it works, pretty much.

205
00:17:14,210 --> 00:17:25,740
[nm]: So, in terms of meditation, again, you don't have to start with some excessively complex topics, you know, relationship between nāmarūpa and viññāna, and viññāna and nāmarūpa, and so on. Just...

206
00:17:27,200 --> 00:17:37,160
[nm]: Just pick up things you think you know, and then really investigate, and by that I mean contemplate rightly, not just think about information you have about it.

207
00:17:37,890 --> 00:17:40,880
[nm]: So, pick up things you know and see whether you really know them.

208
00:17:42,700 --> 00:17:44,720
[nm]: So, don't pull any punches. Don't...

209
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:46,800
[nm]: Don't be afraid of what you might discover.

210
00:17:48,200 --> 00:17:59,960
[nm]: But, like, really dedicate yourself to its clarifying the meaning, which means make yourself open to possibility of doubt, which can be very unpleasant.

211
00:18:00,970 --> 00:18:03,960
[nm]: The way you're going to deal with that doubt is...

212
00:18:04,670 --> 00:18:10,130
[nm]: Well, it's going to be self-evident, if you had... if you have had your sīla established beforehand.

213
00:18:10,140 --> 00:18:19,470
[nm]: If your virtue... if you've been living virtuously and you recognize the necessity of virtue, well, that will take care of the the unpleasantness of doubt and restlessness and so on.

214
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:21,640
[nm]: But you have to go through with it.

215
00:18:22,530 --> 00:18:29,110
[nm]: So, eh... the longer a person who wants to practice dhamma avoids it, eh... the harder it's going to be to do it later on.

216
00:18:31,030 --> 00:18:34,210
[nm]: So, you can just... just eh... just ask yourself.

217
00:18:37,340 --> 00:18:39,190
[nm]: About things you think you understood.

218
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:45,670
[nm]: Whatever that is, whatever sutra you read, you think it made sense, question it.

219
00:18:48,210 --> 00:18:52,370
[nm]: Or even ask yourself what you want to... what you want to... what do you want to get out of the practice?

220
00:18:52,840 --> 00:18:53,950
[nm]: Like you want peace.

221
00:18:54,580 --> 00:18:55,860
[nm]: How would you define peace?

222
00:18:56,170 --> 00:18:58,820
[nm]: Do you have clarity in regard to what peace means?

223
00:18:59,670 --> 00:19:00,830
[nm]: Here we are, I want to be peaceful.

224
00:19:00,830 --> 00:19:01,320
[nm]: What does that mean?

225
00:19:01,450 --> 00:19:03,880
[nm]: Peaceful means non-disturbed.

226
00:19:06,100 --> 00:19:07,620
[nm]: Okay, so take it step further.

227
00:19:08,850 --> 00:19:10,240
[nm]: What is the disturbance then?

228
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:13,260
[nm]: How are you disturbed?

229
00:19:15,020 --> 00:19:18,210
[nm]: Are you disturbed when you experience things you don't want to experience?

230
00:19:19,040 --> 00:19:22,770
[nm]: Are you disturbed when you don't experience things you want to experience?

231
00:19:24,220 --> 00:19:26,920
[nm]: What's in common to any form of disturbance?

232
00:19:27,390 --> 00:19:28,410
[nm]: The common is suffering.

233
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:29,950
[nm]: It's unpleasant.

234
00:19:30,420 --> 00:19:33,670
[nm]: If disturbance is not unpleasant, it wouldn't be a disturbance.

235
00:19:34,290 --> 00:19:43,310
[nm]: So, then, you want peace, which is freedom from disturbance, which means peace is freedom from unpleasant feeling.

236
00:19:43,940 --> 00:19:45,140
[nm]: So, freedom from suffering.

237
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:49,170
[nm]: So, peace equals absence of suffering altogether.

238
00:19:50,740 --> 00:19:52,390
[nm]: Now, you can say, of course, that makes sense.

239
00:19:52,840 --> 00:19:59,340
[nm]: But, by going through these motions of, of, of, like, reflexive drilling, you are actually clarifying it layer by layer.

240
00:19:59,340 --> 00:20:00,140
[nm]: So, next time,

241
00:20:00,690 --> 00:20:09,030
[nm]: When something bothers you, you suffer on account of it, you realize, well, I'm not automatically disturbed by things that bother me.

242
00:20:09,740 --> 00:20:13,790
[nm]: I'm only disturbed when I start suffering on account of things that bother me.

243
00:20:15,190 --> 00:20:18,640
[nm]: So, peace is not... not having anything come your way.

244
00:20:19,610 --> 00:20:25,360
[nm]: Peace is about not being disturbed, not suffer on account of whatever comes your way.

245
00:20:26,050 --> 00:20:29,690
[nm]: Ultimately, sickness, aging and death will come your way.

246
00:20:29,700 --> 00:20:31,040
[nm]: That's not negotiable.

247
00:20:33,140 --> 00:20:37,070
[nm]: So, these little layers that you will be undoing through reflection, that's all it matters.

248
00:20:37,080 --> 00:20:39,960
[nm]: That's how you practice gradually, bit by bit by bit.

249
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:43,960
[nm]: So then, again, other suttas will start making more sense as well.

250
00:20:43,963 --> 00:20:53,474
[nm]: So, when the Buddha says that the root of suffering is your own craving, not the conditions in regard to which you crave, that you realize, okay, so you end up...

251
00:20:54,800 --> 00:20:58,825
[nm]: You're affected by disturbance of any kind, because you disturb yourself.

252
00:20:59,380 --> 00:20:59,954
[nm]: Nobody else.

253
00:20:59,950 --> 00:21:00,667
[nm]: Nothing else.

254
00:21:01,579 --> 00:21:07,643
[nm]: And that can sound almost, well, quite blunt, in a way.

255
00:21:07,820 --> 00:21:11,072
[nm]: But at the same time, it's the best possible news you can receive.

256
00:21:11,072 --> 00:21:16,685
[nm]: Because if suffering were truly rooted in circumstances or other people's actions, you can't control that.

257
00:21:16,690 --> 00:21:17,842
[nm]: You can't even access that.

258
00:21:17,850 --> 00:21:19,456
[nm]: So you wouldn't be able to free yourself from it.

259
00:21:19,995 --> 00:21:20,610
[nm]: But it isn't.

260
00:21:21,122 --> 00:21:22,146
[nm]: And that's the good news.

261
00:21:23,270 --> 00:21:28,939
[nm]: So, the Buddha's teaching should be really considered like a very strict form of optimism.

262
00:21:29,241 --> 00:21:33,584
[nm]: It's recognizing the problem and knowing exactly what to do about it.

263
00:21:34,416 --> 00:21:43,931
[nm]: So, when you recognize that the problem of you being disturbed by things which make you non-peaceful, which is a problem, which is what you don't want, lies in you craving.

264
00:21:44,699 --> 00:21:45,545
[nm]: Craving for what?

265
00:21:46,016 --> 00:21:52,482
[nm]: Craving for the presently pleasant feeling to stay longer, or simply to stay.

266
00:21:54,617 --> 00:22:05,229
[nm]: Craving for the pleasant, presently unpleasant feeling to go away, as soon as possible, and craving for the neutral feeling to be ignored.

267
00:22:06,210 --> 00:22:12,569
[nm]: Practically, those are the three attitudes that perpetuate your liability to suffering.

268
00:22:12,973 --> 00:22:18,667
[nm]: So, in order to stop craving in reality these feelings, you have to learn how to discern these feelings for what they are.

269
00:22:18,910 --> 00:22:20,410
[nm]: And that brings us to the point we discussed.

270
00:22:20,822 --> 00:22:21,570
[nm]: Feeling, feeling.

271
00:22:21,570 --> 00:22:22,370
[nm]: What is feeling?

272
00:22:23,110 --> 00:22:25,339
[nm]: Do I... when I say feeling, I feel this.

273
00:22:25,790 --> 00:22:26,683
[nm]: What am I referring to?

274
00:22:26,690 --> 00:22:28,102
[nm]: What is the phenomenon?

275
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:34,630
[nm]: What is the thing present in my experience as a whole, that I designate by saying feeling?

276
00:22:35,430 --> 00:22:36,194
[nm]: I feel pleasure.

277
00:22:36,200 --> 00:22:36,637
[nm]: What is it?

278
00:22:36,950 --> 00:22:38,692
[nm]: How would you describe your pleasant feeling?

279
00:22:39,400 --> 00:22:41,899
[nm]: Don't necessarily go answering it or describe it like this.

280
00:22:42,317 --> 00:22:45,824
[nm]: Just pause, ask yourself the question, let the question endure.

281
00:22:48,190 --> 00:22:50,985
[nm]: For as long as you... if your mind starts wandering away, ask the question again.

282
00:22:51,641 --> 00:22:55,707
[nm]: And then, try to think about it a little bit more, but don't necessarily rush into an answer.

283
00:22:56,304 --> 00:22:56,592
[nm]: Why?

284
00:22:56,592 --> 00:22:58,998
[nm]: It's because you don't want to be producing the answer.

285
00:22:59,440 --> 00:23:00,496
[nm]: You want to be discerning it.

286
00:23:00,493 --> 00:23:00,681
[nm]: Why?

287
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:02,802
[nm]: Because that present feeling is present.

288
00:23:04,580 --> 00:23:07,885
[nm]: You don't need to answer it in order for that feeling to be there.

289
00:23:08,250 --> 00:23:10,971
[nm]: The fact that you were able to designate it means it's there.

290
00:23:12,070 --> 00:23:15,035
[nm]: But it's your preconceived notions about the feeling

291
00:23:15,710 --> 00:23:18,278
[nm]: that's what obscures you understanding its nature.

292
00:23:18,650 --> 00:23:28,301
[nm]: That's what Mūlapariyāyasutta, it describes that all the conceivings you do on account of the arisen experience, not before the arisen experience.

293
00:23:29,892 --> 00:23:36,246
[nm]: So, it's clearing out of misconceivings or conceivings, and discerning it for what it is. 

294
00:23:36,473 --> 00:23:41,812
[nm]: If you can discern the nature, the presently enduring feeling for what it is, you will be unable to crave it.

295
00:23:43,100 --> 00:23:47,680
[nm]: That's why understanding automatically results in freedom from assumption and craving.

